{"concepts_used":["GC-0001","GC-0002","GC-0009","GC-0010","GC-0011","GC-0017","GC-0019","GC-0038","GC-0072"],"decline_reason":"","declined":false,"citations":[{"ref":"TMM/Part29/2/2/2","quote":"A connotation may result from the whole trade mark or from a part of the trade mark. It may result from a dictionary meaning or from a connection in the minds of the buying public with some person, place or thing."},{"ref":"TMM/Part29/9/9/3~1","quote":"A trade mark containing a standalone geographical reference will cause confusion if each of the following criteria are met: Consumers would have reason to associate the goods/services with the geographical location; The association between the place and the goods/services would influence the purchasing decision of an average consumer of the goods/services. There are no other elements in the mark, or surrounding circumstances, which would mitigate the risk of confusion."},{"ref":"TMM/Part29/9/9/3~2","quote":"Although the trade mark contains a reference to Tuscany, the word EFFECT changes the connotation of the trade mark. Consumers are not likely to think the paint originates from Tuscany, but rather that the paint will give a surface a Tuscan-style finish."},{"ref":"TMA1995/s43","quote":"An application for the registration of a trade mark in respect of particular goods or services must be rejected if, because of some connotation that the trade mark or a sign contained in the trade mark has, the use of the trade mark in relation to those goods or services would be likely to deceive or cause confusion."}],"answer":"**Manual practice — who is affected?** The relevant people are the buying public for the goods or services: the Manual asks whether the place association would influence an average consumer’s purchasing decision. The implied meaning, or ‘connotation’, can arise from the whole mark or just part of it. [TMM/Part29/2/2/2; TMM/Part29/9/9/3~1]

**Manual practice — what changes in shoppers’ perceptions?** A place reference can suggest geographical origin, but other wording can change that impression. In the Manual’s TUSCAN EFFECT paint example, EFFECT shifts the meaning from paint originating in Tuscany to paint producing a Tuscan-style finish. For a standalone place reference, the Manual’s confusion criteria require a reason to associate the goods or services with the place, an influence on purchasing decisions, and no other mark elements or surrounding circumstances mitigating the risk. [TMM/Part29/9/9/3~2; TMM/Part29/9/9/3~1]

**Act — legal requirement:** Section 43 requires rejection where use of the mark for the particular goods or services would, because of its connotation, be likely to deceive or cause confusion. The geographical-reference criteria above are Manual practice, not the wording of the Act. [TMA1995/s43; TMM/Part29/9/9/3~1]"}
